-- Q1: medium / L3 — Retroactive adjustment of prior-period EPS for stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Corp. reported basic EPS of $3.00 for Year 1 based on 100,000 weighted-average shares. On March 1 of Year 2, Waverly declared a 2-for-1 stock split. When presenting comparative financial statements for Year 1 alongside Year 2, what restated basic EPS should Waverly report for Year 1?', '["$3.00, unchanged from the original report", "$6.00, adjusted upward for the split", "$1.50, adjusted retroactively for the 2-for-1 split", "$2.25, adjusted using a partial-year weighting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits are applied retroactively to all prior periods presented in comparative statements. The Year 1 weighted-average shares double to 200,000, restating EPS from $3.00 to $1.50 ($300,000 / 200,000). Reporting the original $3.00 would impair comparability by using inconsistent share bases across periods.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Participating securities and two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fenwick Inc. has 600,000 common shares and 150,000 shares of participating preferred stock that shares ratably in dividends beyond its base rate. Net income is $900,000. Dividends declared total $150,000 ($30,000 to preferred, $120,000 to common). Under the two-class method required by ASC 260, what is basic EPS for common shareholders?', '["$1.45", "$1.20", "$1.50", "$1.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, participating securities require the two-class method. Undistributed earnings: $900,000 − $150,000 = $750,000. Common allocation: 600,000 / 750,000 × $750,000 = $600,000. Common EPS: ($120,000 + $600,000) / 600,000 = $1.20. Simply deducting preferred dividends ($1.45) ignores the preferred stock''s participation right in undistributed earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of simple capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what defines a simple capital structure?', '["The entity has only common stock outstanding with no potentially dilutive securities", "The entity has fewer than three classes of equity securities outstanding", "The entity reports basic and diluted EPS on the income statement", "The entity has no preferred stock in its capital structure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 260-10-15-2 defines a simple capital structure as one with only common stock outstanding and no securities that could dilute EPS upon conversion or exercise. Entities with simple capital structures present only basic EPS. Having preferred stock does not create a complex structure unless the preferred is convertible or otherwise potentially dilutive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Options exercised mid-year weighted treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greystone Corp. has 200,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On July 1, employees exercise 12,000 stock options and Greystone issues 12,000 new shares. For purposes of calculating weighted-average shares for basic EPS, how many shares should Greystone include for the full year?', '["212,000", "200,000", "203,000", "206,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-5, shares issued upon exercise of stock options are included in the weighted-average from their issuance date. The 12,000 shares issued July 1 are weighted for six months: 12,000 × 6/12 = 6,000 incremental shares. Weighted-average: 200,000 + 6,000 = 206,000. Using 212,000 would incorrectly treat the new shares as outstanding for the entire year.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — EPS formula denominator definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the denominator used in the basic earnings per share calculation?', '["Total shares authorized by the corporate charter", "Weighted-average common shares outstanding during the period", "Shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "Total shares issued including treasury shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-30-5 requires the denominator in basic EPS to be the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period. This measure captures the time-weighted effect of share issuances and repurchases throughout the year. Using end-of-period shares would overstate or understate the denominator when shares were issued or repurchased during the year.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted: numerator add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harrington Inc. has net income of $400,000 and 100,000 weighted-average common shares. It also has 5,000 shares of $100 par, 7% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 5 common shares. Under the if-converted method for diluted EPS, what adjustment is made to the numerator?', '["Subtract the $35,000 preferred dividend from net income", "Add the after-tax interest savings of $35,000 to the numerator", "Add back the $35,000 preferred dividend that was deducted in basic EPS", "No adjustment because preferred dividends are not tax-deductible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock assumes conversion at the beginning of the period. The preferred dividends ($5,000 × $100 × 7% = $35,000) deducted in the basic EPS numerator are added back because no preferred dividends would be paid if converted. Unlike convertible debt, there is no tax adjustment because preferred dividends are not tax-deductible.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Restricted stock units in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearfield Corp. grants 20,000 restricted stock units to executives on January 1. The average unrecognized compensation cost during the year is $300,000 and the average market price of Clearfield''s stock is $50 per share. Using the treasury stock method under ASC 260, how many incremental shares from the RSUs are included in diluted EPS?', '["14,000", "20,000", "6,000", "0"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, unvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds equal the average unrecognized compensation cost of $300,000. Shares assumed repurchased at the average market price: $300,000 / $50 = 6,000. Incremental shares: 20,000 − 6,000 = 14,000. Using all 20,000 shares ignores the compensation cost component required by the treasury stock method.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under GAAP", "All entities with more than $10 million in total assets", "Only entities with publicly traded preferred stock", "Only entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 260-10-15-2 limits the EPS presentation requirement to entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS, although they may voluntarily do so. The requirement is based specifically on whether common equity instruments are publicly traded, not on asset size or preferred stock status.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Actual conversion of convertible preferred during the year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarwood Corp. had 10,000 shares of convertible preferred stock outstanding on January 1. On April 1, all preferred shares were converted into 40,000 common shares. Briarwood had 200,000 common shares outstanding before the conversion. For basic EPS, how are the converted shares treated in the weighted-average?', '["Include 40,000 common shares for the entire year", "Include 40,000 common shares from April 1 weighted for nine months", "Exclude the converted shares entirely from basic EPS", "Include 40,000 common shares for three months only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-5, when convertible preferred stock is actually converted, the resulting common shares are included in the weighted-average from the conversion date. The 40,000 shares are weighted for nine months (April through December). Including them for the full year would be incorrect — full-year treatment only applies under the if-converted method for diluted EPS, not basic EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Antidilutive convertible bonds identification
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. reports basic EPS of $2.00 based on net income of $200,000 and 100,000 weighted-average shares. The company has $500,000 face value of 10% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 20 shares. The tax rate is 25%. What is the per-share impact of the bonds, and are they dilutive or antidilutive?', '["$3.75 per share; dilutive", "$2.50 per share; dilutive", "$3.75 per share; antidilutive", "$2.00 per share; neither dilutive nor antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible bonds are tested by computing per-share impact: after-tax interest ($50,000 × 0.75 = $37,500) divided by incremental shares (500 bonds × 20 = 10,000) = $3.75. Because $3.75 exceeds basic EPS of $2.00, including these bonds would increase EPS, making them antidilutive. Antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS per ASC 260-10-45-17.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Definition of complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What characterizes a complex capital structure under ASC 260?', '["The entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute earnings per share", "The entity has more than two classes of stock authorized in its charter", "The entity reports three or more subtotals on its income statement", "The entity operates in both domestic and international markets"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding potentially dilutive securities such as convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock, stock options, or warrants. Entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS. The number of authorized stock classes or operational scope does not determine structural complexity for EPS purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Written put options effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Millbrook Corp. has 100,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $300,000 (basic EPS of $3.00). The company wrote put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $60 when the average market price was $40. Under the reverse treasury stock method in ASC 260, how many incremental shares result from the written puts?', '["10,000", "0", "4,000", "5,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options with an exercise price above the average market price are dilutive. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity issues shares at the $40 market price to fund the $600,000 repurchase (15,000 shares issued), then retires the 10,000 repurchased shares. Net incremental shares: 15,000 − 10,000 = 5,000. Using 10,000 ignores the share issuance assumption.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — EPS for discontinued operations presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summerlin Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $800,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $200,000 (net of tax), yielding net income of $600,000 with 200,000 weighted-average shares and no preferred stock. How should EPS be presented on the income statement?', '["Only net income EPS of $3.00", "EPS of $4.00 for continuing operations and ($1.00) for discontinued operations", "EPS of $3.00 for continuing operations with a footnote for discontinued operations", "Combined EPS of $3.00 with no separate line items required"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 260-10-45-3 requires entities to present EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS for discontinued operations ($200,000 / 200,000 = $1.00 loss per share) must also be disclosed either on the face or in the notes. Reporting only net income EPS would conceal the impact of discontinued operations on per-share performance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Dilutive securities ranking order
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Ltd. has basic EPS of $5.00 ($500,000 net income, 100,000 shares). It has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options adding 5,000 incremental shares, (2) 6% convertible bonds adding $18,000 after-tax interest and 12,000 shares, and (3) convertible preferred stock adding $30,000 dividends and 10,000 shares. Under the dilutive-securities ranking approach in ASC 260, what is the correct order of inclusion?', '["Bonds, preferred, options", "Options, preferred, bonds", "Options, bonds, preferred", "Preferred, bonds, options"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, potentially dilutive securities are ranked from most dilutive (lowest per-share impact) to least dilutive. Options always rank first ($0 numerator impact). Bonds: $18,000 / 12,000 = $1.50 per share. Preferred: $30,000 / 10,000 = $3.00 per share. The correct order is options, bonds, then preferred. Ranking preferred before bonds would incorrectly place the least dilutive security first.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Shares issued as stock compensation mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Inc. has 300,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On September 1, it issues 12,000 shares to employees as part of a vested stock bonus plan. No other share activity occurs during the year. What is the weighted-average number of shares for basic EPS?', '["304,000", "306,000", "312,000", "300,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-5, newly issued shares are included in the weighted-average from their issuance date. The 12,000 bonus shares issued September 1 are weighted for four months (September through December): 12,000 × 4/12 = 4,000. Total weighted-average: 300,000 + 4,000 = 304,000. Using 312,000 would incorrectly treat the bonus shares as outstanding for the full year.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Two-class method allocation calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgecrest Corp. has 400,000 common shares and 100,000 shares of Series A participating preferred stock. Net income is $750,000. Preferred stockholders receive a $0.50 per-share annual dividend before participating ratably with common in remaining earnings. Total dividends declared are $250,000 ($50,000 to preferred, $200,000 to common). Under the two-class method, what is basic EPS for common shareholders?', '["$1.75", "$1.88", "$1.25", "$1.50"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings proportionally. Undistributed earnings: $750,000 − $250,000 = $500,000. Common allocation: 400,000 / (400,000 + 100,000) × $500,000 = $400,000. Common EPS: ($200,000 + $400,000) / 400,000 = $1.50. Simply deducting preferred dividends ($1.75) ignores the preferred stock''s participation right in undistributed earnings, overstating common EPS.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Dual EPS presentation requirement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookhaven Corp. has common stock traded on a national exchange and convertible bonds outstanding. The controller is preparing the annual income statement. Under ASC 260, what EPS figures must Brookhaven present?', '["Both basic EPS and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement", "Only basic EPS on the face with diluted EPS disclosed in the notes", "Only diluted EPS since it is the more conservative measure", "Basic EPS only because diluted EPS is optional for all entities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 260-10-45-2 requires entities with complex capital structures to present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement. Presenting only diluted EPS or relegating either measure to the footnotes does not satisfy the dual-presentation requirement for publicly traded entities with potentially dilutive securities outstanding.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Retroactive treatment of stock dividends on prior-period EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Inc. reported basic EPS of $2.40 in Year 1. On February 15 of Year 2, Westbrook declared a 20% stock dividend. When presenting Year 2 comparative statements, how should Year 1 EPS be reported?', '["$2.40, with a footnote about the stock dividend", "$2.88, adjusted upward for the additional shares", "$2.00, adjusted retroactively for the 20% stock dividend", "$2.40, because prior-period EPS is never restated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends must be applied retroactively to all prior periods presented in comparative statements. Weighted-average shares for Year 1 increase by 20%, reducing Year 1 EPS from $2.40 to $2.00 ($2.40 / 1.20). Reporting the original $2.40 would impair comparability between periods by using inconsistent share bases across reporting years.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Diluted EPS with both options and convertible preferred combined
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Edgewater Inc. reports net income of $660,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares and $60,000 in cumulative preferred dividends (basic EPS of $3.00). The company has 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $20 (average market price $40), and the preferred stock is convertible into 30,000 common shares. Using proper sequencing under ASC 260, what is Edgewater''s diluted EPS?', '["$2.86", "$2.75", "$3.00", "$2.50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45, dilutive securities are included from most to least dilutive. Options: 20,000 issued − 10,000 repurchased ($200,000/$40) = 10,000 incremental shares. After options: $600,000 / 210,000 = $2.86. Convertible preferred per-share impact: $60,000 / 30,000 = $2.00, below $2.86, so dilutive. Diluted EPS: ($600,000 + $60,000) / (210,000 + 30,000) = $2.75. Stopping after options ($2.86) understates dilution.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — EPS must be reported for continuing operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which income statement line item must EPS always be presented on the face of the statement?', '["Gross profit", "Operating income", "Comprehensive income", "Income from continuing operations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 260-10-45-3 requires EPS presentation for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS for subtotals such as gross profit, operating income, or comprehensive income is not required by the standard. This ensures investors can evaluate per-share profitability from ongoing core operations separately from discontinued items.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — At-the-money options have zero dilutive effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Corp. has 150,000 shares outstanding and net income of $450,000. The company has 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $35, and the average market price for the year is $35. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$2.76", "$3.00", "$2.50", "$2.86"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes options are exercised and proceeds repurchase shares at the average market price. When the exercise price equals the market price ($35), proceeds repurchase exactly 10,000 shares, producing zero incremental shares. At-the-money options have no dilutive effect, so diluted EPS equals basic EPS of $3.00 ($450,000 / 150,000).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Purpose of treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the purpose of the treasury stock method in EPS calculations under ASC 260?', '["To determine the number of shares the entity should hold as treasury stock", "To calculate the gain or loss on treasury stock transactions during the period", "To compute the tax benefit from employee stock option exercises", "To measure the dilutive effect of stock options and warrants on earnings per share"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method determines the dilutive impact of stock options and warrants by assuming exercise proceeds are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price. The net incremental shares are added to the denominator for diluted EPS. Convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock use the if-converted method, not the treasury stock method.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Share repurchase mid-year effect on weighted-average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplewood Corp. has 250,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On October 1, the company repurchases 30,000 shares as treasury stock. No other share activity occurs during the year. What is the weighted-average number of shares for basic EPS?', '["242,500", "250,000", "220,000", "235,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-5, treasury stock repurchases reduce the weighted-average from the repurchase date. The 30,000 shares are excluded for three months (October through December): 30,000 × 3/12 = 7,500. Weighted-average: 250,000 − 7,500 = 242,500. Using 250,000 ignores the repurchase, while 220,000 fails to weight the reduction for only the three months it was in effect.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Potentially dilutive security example
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which of the following is an example of a potentially dilutive security under ASC 260?', '["Nonconvertible preferred stock", "Treasury stock held by the issuing entity", "An account receivable from a related party", "A stock option granted to an employee"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, potentially dilutive securities are instruments that may reduce EPS upon exercise or conversion, including stock options, warrants, convertible bonds, and convertible preferred stock. Nonconvertible preferred stock reduces the basic EPS numerator through dividends but cannot create additional common shares. Treasury stock and receivables have no dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Contingently issuable shares condition not met for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Inc. has 100,000 weighted-average shares and $400,000 net income. The company agreed to issue 20,000 additional shares to former owners of an acquired business if cumulative revenue exceeds $5 million within three years. At year-end, cumulative revenue is $3.2 million. How are these contingent shares treated for basic EPS?', '["Include all 20,000 shares in the weighted-average", "Include 12,800 shares proportional to the revenue achieved", "Exclude the shares entirely from basic EPS", "Include 20,000 shares but disclose them as contingent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all necessary conditions are satisfied. Because cumulative revenue of $3.2 million has not reached the $5 million threshold, the condition is unmet and the shares are excluded from basic EPS. Including proportional shares or the full 20,000 would violate the all-or-nothing treatment required by the standard.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Numerator for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the numerator in the basic earnings per share formula under ASC 260?', '["Net income minus preferred dividends", "Net income plus interest expense", "Gross profit minus operating expenses", "Operating income minus income tax expense"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, the numerator for basic EPS is net income available to common shareholders, which equals net income minus preferred dividends (whether declared or, for cumulative preferred, accumulated). Interest expense is not added back for basic EPS — that adjustment applies only to the if-converted method for convertible debt in diluted EPS calculations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Convertible debt: after-tax interest in if-converted numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. has $1,000,000 of 8% convertible bonds outstanding for the entire year. The corporate tax rate is 25%. Under the if-converted method for diluted EPS, what amount is added to the numerator to reflect the assumed conversion of these bonds?', '["$80,000", "$60,000", "$20,000", "$100,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes bonds were converted at the beginning of the period, eliminating interest expense. The numerator increases by the after-tax interest savings: $80,000 × (1 − 0.25) = $60,000. Using the full $80,000 ignores the tax effect — since interest is tax-deductible, removing it also eliminates the associated tax shield, requiring an after-tax adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Interim period EPS: loss quarter excludes dilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. reports quarterly results for Year 1: Q1 income $120,000, Q2 income $180,000, Q3 loss ($60,000), Q4 income $160,000. Weighted-average shares are 100,000 each quarter with 5,000 dilutive stock options. For the Q3 interim report under ASC 260, how should Crestview compute diluted EPS for the third quarter?', '["Include the options because year-to-date income through Q3 is positive", "Include the options since each quarter uses the same dilutive securities", "Average the quarterly diluted EPS figures for the annual presentation", "Exclude the options because including them would be antidilutive for Q3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, each interim period is evaluated independently for dilution. In Q3, Crestview reports a net loss, so all potentially dilutive securities become antidilutive because adding shares to the denominator would reduce the loss per share. The options must be excluded from Q3 diluted EPS regardless of year-to-date profitability. Cumulative income cannot override the quarterly loss test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Participating preferred in a net loss year under two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeway Corp. has 400,000 common shares and 100,000 participating preferred shares. In Year 1, Ridgeway reports a net loss of $500,000 and declares no dividends. The preferred shares participate ratably in dividends but are not contractually obligated to absorb losses. Under the two-class method, what is basic EPS for common shareholders?', '["($1.25)", "($1.00)", "($1.50)", "($0.80)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-66, when a participating security does not contractually share in losses, the entire net loss is allocated exclusively to common shareholders under the two-class method. Common EPS: ($500,000) / 400,000 = ($1.25). Allocating losses across all 500,000 shares ($1.00) would incorrectly force the preferred stock to absorb losses despite having no contractual obligation to do so.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Weighted-average with mid-year stock dividend and subsequent issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Corp. has 100,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On July 1, it declares a 25% stock dividend. On October 1, it issues 10,000 shares for cash. What is the weighted-average number of shares for the year?', '["135,000", "132,500", "127,500", "137,500"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively to all shares outstanding before the dividend. Pre-dividend shares become 125,000 (100,000 × 1.25), treated as outstanding all year. The October cash issuance is weighted for three months: 10,000 × 3/12 = 2,500. Weighted-average: 125,000 + 2,500 = 127,500. Using 135,000 incorrectly treats the October shares as outstanding for the full year.', 'medium', 2)
;

